%[text] %[text:anchor:H_75237720] ## Convergence study on transverse shear-locking
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_79CBC25A] In this section it is shown how the rate of the mesh convergence study is reduced down due to transverse shear locking. The effect gets more pronounced with increased slenderness of the beam.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1D5027AE] ### Number of elements
numEl = 100; %[control:slider:14e0]{"position":[9,12]}
if floor(numEl) ~= numEl
    error("Variables 'numEl' must be an integer")
end
if numEl <= 0
    error("Variable 'numEl' must be strictly positive")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:8c10) **Try**
%[text] Modify the number of refinement steps by means of variable `numRef`
%[text] ![](text:image:5748) **Reflect**
%[text] - Keep the slenderness of the beam small enough $\\lambda = \\frac{L}{t} \\in \\mathcal{O}(1)$. What is the critical number of elements beyond which no variation in the tip deflection of the beam is observed?
%[text] - Increase the slenderness of the beam to $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{O}(10^{-2})$. What is the critical number of elements beyond which no variation in the tip deflection of the beam is observed? Note that you may need to increase the number of refinements by means of variable `numRef` to observe convergence \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8D3FADBD] ### Convergence study based on the tip displacement of the beam
%[text] Navigate to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter62_HellingerReissnerTimoshenko_ConvergenceStudy.m) section for the detailed analysis.
